in this lesson you're gonna learn how to  find the lateral area surface area and  volume of a pyramid and the first thing  we want to talk about here is the  formulas now the formula for lateral  area the lateral area means the area of  the sides which of course in a pyramid  the size are made up of triangles so we  can use this formula 1/2 perimeter of  the base times the slant height Alice  for like the leaning height of the slant  height that would be five in this  particular example but I just want to  show you where the formula comes from  real quick if you find the area of one  of the triangles which we know is  one-half base times height and we  multiply it by the number of triangles  let's just call that n n times B ok so B  represents the base of the triangle  times a number of triangles that would  actually end up being the perimeter so  that's why n times B we write SP that H  is the height of the triangle but in the  pyramid we call that L or the slant  height so that's where this formula is  coming from right here for the surface  area though we're going to take the  lateral area which is the sides and  we're going to add on the base so when  you see that capital B that's the area  of the base so whatever shape that is if  it's you know a hexagon you have to find  the area of a hexagon if it's a square  you know the find the area of a square  and then lastly the volume one-third of  the area of the base times the height  and what I try to demonstrate here is  that if you were to put a pyramid inside  of a prism if the bases are the same and  the height is the same you could  actually put three of these pyramids  inside of that prism okay so that means  that one of these is going to take up  one-third of the volume so let's go  ahead and work through this problem here  the lateral area is going to be one half  the perimeter of the base times the  slant height the perimeter is going to  be four times 8 which is 32 the slant  height is five you don't want to make  the mistake of using this what they call  lateral edge okay that's where the  lateral faces come together you want the  height of the triangle that's the slant  height so this comes out to 16 times 5  which is 80 units squared remember areas  square units now if we want to find the  surface area we just have to add on that  square on the bottom 8 times 8 is 64  that's the area of the base so if we add  on 64 we're gonna get a hundred  forty-four units squared and then for  the volume we're just gonna do one-third  the area of the base times the height  the area of the base is sixty-four times  the height oh we don't know the height  so how are we gonna find that height  well what you can do is you can make a  triangle like this so if this side  length is eight that means this must be  half it's four and then you can see that  this is a triangle here a right triangle  we can use the Pythagorean theorem or if  you recognize your Pythagorean triples  this is going to be a three four five  Pythagorean triple which makes that  height three of course you can use the  Pythagorean theorem as well one-third  times 3 is 1 times 64 is 64 so we get 64  units cubed for the volume now let's do  a more challenging one that involves  some trigonometry and let's do that one  next okay number two we have a  pentagonal pyramid because the base is a  five sided regular polygon and we want  to find out what exactly is the lateral  area surface area and volume see if you  can do this one on your own and if you  get it right let me know in the comments  below because I I definitely made this  one more challenging but the first thing  I would do here is I'd recognize that  they're giving us the overall height  which is H they're giving us the slant  height which is our L which is 13 and if  we do our Pythagorean theorem we can  find out what this length is right here  and that's going to be 5 it's a 5 12 13  Pythagorean triple now let's take a look  at this base let's see if we can draw it  a little bit bigger here off to the side  now this 5 right here see how it's the  perpendicular distance from the centre  of the Pentagon to the side that's our  apothem  so this is going to be 5 right here and  remember when you worked with regular  polygons meaning all the sides are  congruent all the angles are congruent  you can find this central angle by  taking 360 divided by 5 which is 72  degrees when we drop the altitude it's  going to bisect that central angle  that's going to be 36 degrees now we can  find out what the length of this side is  okay so let's see what is that going to  be let's just call this X so we've got  opposite and adjacent that's tangent so  we have the tangent of 36 degrees  opposite over adjacent if we multiply  both sides by five you can see that x  equals five times the tangent of 36 and  let me see what that is on the  calculator I'm gonna round a little bit  here so it's about three point six three  now if I double that to get the length  of the side that comes out to about  seven point two seven just rounding here  and if we want to find the perimeter we  would take that seven point three seven  and multiply by five so that's about  thirty six point three three okay for  the perimeter but now what we want to do  is we want to find the lateral area okay  of this pyramid so lateral area is equal  to one half the perimeter which is  thirty six point three three times the  slant height which is the L the leaning  height here which is thirteen so let's  multiply that out times thirteen times  1/2 so we're looking at a lateral area  of two hundred thirty six point one  three units squared if we want to find  the surface area which is the total area  so we have to add on that base we're  gonna take the lateral area which we  just found plus the area of this  Pentagon what's the formula for area of  a Pentagon  well regular polygon we use the formula  one-half apothem times perimeter the  apothem we said was five  that's this perpendicular distance from  the center to the side and the perimeter  is going to be seven point two seven  times five which we said was thirty-six  point three three so let's see if we can  multiply that out thirty-six point three  three times 0.5 times five and then  that's about ninety point eight three  and we're gonna add it to the two  hundred thirty six point one three so  this is about okay I'm rounding here so  it's about three hundred twenty six  point nine six units squared that's for  our surface area now for the volume we  have to use our form the one-third area  of the base times the height well we  found the base which was the ninety  point eight three so it's going to be  one-third times ninety point eight three  times the height now you want to make  sure you use the overall height  the volume not the slant height so this  height is going to be 12 and 1/3 times  12 is 4 times the 90.8 3 so it looks  like it's about three hundred and sixty  three point three two units cubed for  volume and you got it if you want to see  more examples with like cylinders and  prisms and spheres and all different  shapes using lateral area surface area  and volume follow me over to that more  comprehensive video where we go through  all these different polyhedrons their  areas volumes shapes etc and how the  formulas relate to one another to help  you memorize them more easily so I'll  see you in that video right there

